<p>Israel’s military has dropped an indictment against five reservists accused of sexually abusing a Palestinian detainee at the Sde Teiman detention facility — a case that drew wide attention in Israel and abroad. Military prosecutors said they no longer believe they can prove the case beyond a reasonable doubt, pointing to missing key evidence and “exceptional circumstances,” including procedural problems over how information was handled and the earlier leak of video from the case. The alleged abuse dates to July 2024, during the Gaza war. A video surfaced that appeared to show soldiers assaulting a blindfolded detainee; multiple reports say the man was later hospitalized with severe injuries. The decision has reopened debate in Israel over accountability and how Palestinian detainees are treated, with some officials welcoming the case’s closure and human rights groups warning it could deepen a sense of impunity.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/J3bZqpFRrBZfBSHLbi_RTKoBofLkzTV3mPZjoE-Cx1TbHIOTuVFeJl6rpP0da4QAAwH6c7eSfAZINGrTAeA7PelnOTWrk3fizmRHFZPv4CA2LlLYITXuTxwuzbosz3HOtP0h1e8u9hiktnseUb1D7jw-RkU-EJbY-OwaQDcV9f7GuVAz2C81HhYpHRfb88MQoikoSrbbb_k-lBz4VxVzfWNZYmRH2fhxXChWawjalRO3p_f7P827ht3XgH7gEaFIk1cl3q209fCzt2thgA' alt='A protester waves an Israeli flag in support of soldiers being questioned for detainee abuse outside Sde Teiman base in July 2024 (file photo).' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Decision-maker and rationale: The military advocate general, Itay Offir, ordered prosecutors to withdraw the indictment. He said the victim’s return to Gaza left prosecutors without key evidence, and that the handling of information — including problems after the video leak and other procedural issues — raised fair-trial concerns.</li><li>What prosecutors alleged: When they filed charges, the army said the soldiers used severe violence, including stabbing the detainee’s buttocks with a sharp object that penetrated near his rectum.</li><li>Political and civil-society fallout: Netanyahu welcomed the decision, saying it was unacceptable that the case took so long to close. Rights advocates condemned the move, including warnings that it could give soldiers “license to rape” detainees.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>IDF military prosecutors: They said they dropped the indictment because they no longer believed they could meet the evidentiary bar for a conviction, and because procedural issues and other “exceptional circumstances” hurt the chances of a fair trial. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cm2xrz71zm3o'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu: He welcomed the decision to close the case, saying it was unacceptable that it took so long. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1981395/israel-drops-indictment-of-five-soldiers-accused-of-sexually-assaulting-palestinian-detainee'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Israeli human rights groups: They said ending the case signals tolerance for mistreatment in detention and warned it could encourage further abuse. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/israeli-military-drops-charges-against-soldiers-accused-of-sexually-assaulting-palestinian-detainee/article70738120.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Israeli society (public debate): The details of the alleged abuse — including reports that the detainee was hospitalized with broken ribs and a torn rectum — intensified arguments over detainee treatment and whether the system fosters impunity during the war. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/12/world/middleeast/israel-detainee-abuse-charges.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/634700-idf-soldiers-palestinian-detainee-abuse/'>IDF drops charges against soldiers accused of sexually assaulting Palestinian detainee</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/12/world/middleeast/israel-detainee-abuse-charges.html'>Israel Drops Case Against Soldiers Accused of Abusing Palestinian Detainee</a> 